Below is a **minimal, self‑contained** C# program that shows the most common ways to work with tuples (`ValueTuple`) in modern C#.  
It covers:

| What it demonstrates | Code snippet |
|-----------------------|--------------|
| Creating a tuple with named elements | `var person = (Name: "Alice", Age: 30);` |
| Accessing elements by name or by position | `person.Name`, `person.Item2` |
| Deconstructing a tuple into local variables | `var (name, age) = person;` |
| Returning a tuple from a method | `static (int min, int max) GetMinMax(int a, int b)` |
| Comparing tuples (value equality & ordering) | `tuple1 == tuple2`, `tuple1.CompareTo(tuple2)` |

```csharp
using System;

namespace TupleDemo
{
    class Program
    {
        static void Main()
        {
            // 1. Create a tuple with named elements
            var person = (Name: "Alice", Age: 30);
            Console.WriteLine($"Person: {person.Name}, {person.Age}");

            // 2. Access elements by name or by index
            Console.WriteLine($"Name (by name)  : {person.Name}");
            Console.WriteLine($"Name (by index) : {person.Item1}");
            Console.WriteLine($"Age (by name)   : {person.Age}");
            Console.WriteLine($"Age (by index)  : {person.Item2}");

            // 3. Deconstruct a tuple into local variables
            var (name, age) = person;
            Console.WriteLine($"Deconstructed: name = {name}, age = {age}");

            // 4. Return a tuple from a method
            var (min, max) = GetMinMax(42, 17);
            Console.WriteLine($"Min: {min}, Max: {max}");

            // 5. Compare tuples (value equality)
            var t1 = (1, "apple");
            var t2 = (1, "apple");
            var t3 = (2, "banana");
            Console.WriteLine($"t1 == t2? {t1 == t2}");   // true
            Console.WriteLine($"t1 == t3? {t1 == t3}");   // false

            // 6. Compare tuples (ordering)
            Console.WriteLine($"t1.CompareTo(t3) = {t1.CompareTo(t3)}"); // -1 (t1 < t3)
            Console.WriteLine($"t3.CompareTo(t1) = {t3.CompareTo(t1)}"); // 1  (t3 > t1)

            // 7. Nested tuples (tuple inside a tuple)
            var complex = (Person: person, Location: (City: "Seattle", Zip: 98101));
            Console.WriteLine($"{complex.Person.Name} lives in {complex.Location.City} {complex.Location.Zip}");
        }

        // Example method that returns a tuple (named elements)
        static (int min, int max) GetMinMax(int a, int b)
        {
            return a < b ? (a, b) : (b, a);
        }
    }
}
```

### What you’ll see when you run it

```
Person: Alice, 30
Name (by name)  : Alice
Name (by index) : Alice
Age (by name)   : 30
Age (by index)  : 30
Deconstructed: name = Alice, age = 30
Min: 17, Max: 42
t1 == t2? True
t1 == t3? False
t1.CompareTo(t3) = -1
t3.CompareTo(t1) = 1
Alice lives in Seattle 98101
```

Feel free to copy this into a new C# console project (e.g., using `dotnet new console`) and run it.  
It demonstrates the essential syntax and operations you’ll use with tuples in everyday C# code.
